            <div xml:id="d243">

                <pb facs="0709" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v13/pg688"/>

                <head>243. Telegram From the Department of State to the
                        Embassy in France<anchor n="0" xml:id="d243-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1962-03-14T21:25:00-05:00">March 14, 1962,
                            9:25 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>4920. Eyes only from Acting Secretary for Ambassador. Our relations with the
                    French on nuclear questions are obviously at a very low ebb. This has been made
                    clear by your recent conversations with Debre and others, and by Debre’s remarks to the American correspondents. The bitterness
                    shown by the French on this subject makes it plain that very little can be
                    gained by discussing the subject further with the French at this time.</p>
                <p>The President believes therefore that it would be best for the present to avoid
                    being drawn into further discussion of nuclear questions with French officials
                    which could only further exacerbate our bilateral relations generally.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"
                    >Ball</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d243-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d243-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 700.5612/3–1462. Secret. Drafted by
                        Fessenden; cleared by Tyler, Owen, Beigel, and
                        Bundy (in substance); and approved and
                    signed by Ball.</p>
            </note>
